    "explanation": "To the rider and to the driver: you are reading the same gap in the evidence in opposite directions, and neither reading can be reached on this record.\n\nThe uncontested facts are these. The trip record shows a driver 'arrived' event at 12:26:40, six minutes after the 12:20:40 acceptance, and the driver ended the trip at 12:35:40 under reason code RIDER_NO_SHOW, one of the codes POL-NS-01 limb (d) recognises. The fare ledger shows SGD 4.00 charged to the rider at 12:36:00 with SGD 0.00 refunded, and that figure reconciles with the fee schedule, which fixes the charge as the GREATER of the STANDARD zone-class fee (SGD 4.00) and the RYDE_X vehicle-tier fee (SGD 4.00) — so there is no billing-integrity defect under POL-NS-08(d).\n\nWhat the record does not contain is any location data. The GPS trace was never retained: EV-GPS-00626 is MISSING at weight 0.0 with issue MISSING_ARTIFACT. The traffic advisory is missing for the same reason: EV-ADVIS-00626-01, weight 0.0, MISSING_ARTIFACT.\n\nPOL-NS-01 lets a no-show fee stand only if all four of its conditions hold, and the driver must carry every one. Two of them read the GPS trace: limb (b), which needs the trace to place the driver inside the 50 m STANDARD pickup geofence at or before the arrival event, and limb (c), which needs a continuous in-geofence dwell of at least 300 seconds measured from the first qualifying GPS fix — not from the 'arrived' tap and not from the trip record's wait_time_min. With no trace, those two limbs are neither satisfied nor broken. They are undecided, and an undecided limb supports neither the charge nor the refund.\n\nThat is why I cannot grant the rider a full refund on the argument that 'no trace means he never arrived.' POL-NS-02 reaches a full refund where the trace contains no qualifying in-radius fix, but the policy is explicit that a defective trace is evidence of impairment rather than proof of absence, and an absent trace is the limiting case of a defective one. And it is why I cannot let the SGD 4.00 stand, or pay the driver, on the argument that the trip record's 9.0-minute wait_time_min proves the required dwell: limb (c) deliberately runs the clock from the GPS fix, so the record alone cannot carry that fact.\n\nThe driver's compensation claim therefore fails at its own gate. POL-NS-07 pays the driver 80 percent of the fee — SGD 3.20 of SGD 4.00 — only where POL-NS-01 is satisfied in full, and it is not. The anti-farming check POL-NS-07 names does pass (no_show_terminations_30d is 1, below the threshold of 4, and 1 divided by completed_trips_30d of 110 is 0.009, below 0.08), but that check only gates a compensation that has no predicate here. The rider's alternative routes are unavailable too: POL-NS-04 does not apply because the chat log holds zero messages and the driver placed an answered in-app call at 12:32:40, and POL-NS-06 limb (i) cannot be invoked because it requires a present and populated advisory, and EV-ADVIS-00626-01 is missing. No profile-based escalation trigger fires either: disputes_with_counterparty_90d is 0 on both profiles, and rider_profile.no_show_disputes_30d is 0 — so the escalation is not about abuse on either side.\n\nThe one fact this whole dispute turns on — whether the driver was at the pickup and waited the required time — is carried only by an artifact that was not retained. Confidence is therefore capped at 0.0, far below the platform-wide floor of 0.70, and the case goes to a human reviewer under POL-NS-09(a) and POL-NS-09(e). The SGD 4.00 is held: it is neither collected nor refunded while the review is pending.\n\nThe specific question for the reviewer: whether a driver 'arrived' event at 12:26:40 with a recorded 9-minute wait can stand as a verified rider no-show when no GPS trace was retained to test the arrival, and why the trace for this trip is missing at all."
